+*   **Strengths**: High performance on multi-core processors due to efficient memory hierarchy usage and algorithmic coherence. Adaptive block size optimizes for different view distances.
+*   **Limitations**: Performance may drop on highly incoherent scenes if SIMD optimizations are not used, as the method relies on high-level algorithmic techniques rather than instruction-level parallelism [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use**: Interactive rendering of high-resolution volumetric data on multi-core CPUs where coherence can be exploited.
+*   **When Not to Use**: Scenes with extreme incoherence where SIMD-based packet ray casting might be more effective, or on hardware without multi-core capabilities [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced by J. Deng, C. Liu, and M. C. Yuen in "Interactive High Resolution Isosurface Ray Tracing on Multi-Core Processors" [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the need for efficient isosurface rendering on modern multi-core architectures by leveraging a compact BONO data structure and a two-phase approach to minimize redundant ray casting [S1, unofficial]. The BONO (Branch-On-Need Octree) structure was previously introduced in [21] (not cited in the provided sources, but referenced in [S1, unofficial]) [S1, unofficial]. The method was tested on LLNL datasets [S1, unofficial].
+The method was introduced in a paper by researchers at the University of Maryland (UMD) [S1, unofficial]. It was designed to leverage multi-core processor architectures and their memory hierarchies for efficient isosurface rendering. The BONO (Branch-On-Need Octree) structure is a key component, providing a compact indexing structure for the volumetric data [S1, unofficial]. The approach differs from standard packet ray casting by avoiding SIMD instructions, instead relying on list merging and projection-based culling to handle coherence [S1, unofficial].
 
